  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong on envelope independence by design: the envelope only carries pcid and opaque payload, defers meaning to the payload protocol, and fails explicitly on unknown pCID. Weakness: the sim does not show a concrete CAS-backed group-session round-trip across sibling envelope variants, and wrapper-based signatures could still leak envelope-specific semantics.",
    "strengths": [
      "Minimal envelope keeps group-session meaning in the payload protocol.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling preserves exact bytes and makes failures explicit.",
      "Wrapper-based signatures allow evolution without changing the base envelope shape."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No end-to-end proof that different envelope variants preserve identical session outcomes.",
      "Signature and authorship wrappers may still affect application semantics.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, limiting long-term confidence."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Outer-wrapper details could become de facto session semantics.",
      "Auditing requires resolving pCIDs and wrapper stacks, which weakens standalone inspectability."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Can the same CAS-backed session message be wrapped in this variant and a sibling variant with identical membership and ordering outcomes?",
      "What is the canonical signed unit for session meaning: inner payload, outer envelope, or wrapper chain?",
      "How should mixed-version peers handle unknown signature-wrapper pCIDs without breaking session safety?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
